WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.700
When you’re a kid, birthday parties are a big deal.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.840
But, as we get older, they come to symbolize something not-so-fun...

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:08.700
Aging

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.300
Aging isn’t just candles on a cake.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.040
In fact, one of the biggest questions in aging research is...

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.320
what *is* aging?

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.140
Frankly, we don’t really know.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.560
And the answer is different depending on whether you’re looking inside our cells, in our brains, or in other species.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:30.140
Sure, wrinkles, gray hair, and arthritic joints signal aging on the surface.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.680
But its underpinnings remain enigmatic — and controversial.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.820
Still, researchers have found certain symptoms of aging deep inside the body.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.340
Protective caps on the ends of chromosomes called telomeres,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.900
represented here by ribbon on a present,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.780
get shorter and shorter with age.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.500
That leaves DNA more prone to wear and tear.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.060
Patterns of chemical tags on DNA

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.220
that guide cell activity also vary with age.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.880
Levels of different substances in the blood even fluctuate as we get older.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.340
Some scientists think that the aging process is preprogrammed into our DNA.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.000
Others see aging as the build up of small mechanical failures in the body over time.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.660
The brain’s path to old age may mirror how it was built in the first place.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.760
Your brain starts out as a blob of identical cells.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.100
Eventually brain cells take on more specific jobs,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.040
link up to other cells, and form neural networks.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.300
As you get older, these jobs and connections get more and more specialized.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.600
And then, some researchers think, the reverse may happen.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.880
Long-distance connections degrade.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.700
Specialist cells revert back to their generalist ways.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.740
Well-built brains may be more resistant to these changes.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.320
But what about aging in other organisms?

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.280
Biologists often study aging by tracking how mortality rates — the odds that a plant or animal will die

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.800
— change as organisms get older.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.120
That’s called a mortality curve,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.640
and for humans, the end is pretty steep.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.360
But, for some species of tiny pond animals called hydra,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.340
the curve is a actually straight line.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.120
Their odds of dying don’t change as they get older.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.220
Some hydra may not age at all.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.240
Desert tortoises, on the other hand,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.240
are more likely to die when they’re young,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.640
but as they mature, their odds more or less plateau.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.480
So, what is aging?

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.600
It’s the process of growing old,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.760
but that process varies from species to species

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.000
and person to person.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.240
Some people experience a gradual decline before they die.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.200
Others remain perfectly healthy and then quickly succumb to an illness.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.460
Aging might still be a bit of a mystery,

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.720
but there is one thing most researchers agree on:

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.360
The process could be slowed or even stopped.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.780
While death is inevitable, aging doesn’t have to be.

